   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Problem estimating covariance matrix in imputation model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Sorry I missed this the first time around.\r<br>\r<br>Usually you want to scale the correlation matrix into\r<br>a covariance matrix.  There&#39;s a discussion in the manual chapter\r<br>on regression.\r<br>\r<br>Also, I&#39;m not sure why you&#39;re giving the imputed values two\r<br>distributions, one with\r<br>\r<br>&gt;   imps ~ normal(0,2);\r<br>\r<br>and one with:\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;       for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;         y_normal_i[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? imps[missing_counter[n,v]] : y_normal[n,v];\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; ...\r<br>&gt;   y_normal_i ~ multi_normal_cholesky(i_mu, i_L_Omega);\r<br>\r<br>You should probably just get rid of the first one (normal(0, 2)).\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Jan 18, 2017, at 6:06 AM, Guido Biele &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guido...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; The model below imputes missing data from a multivariate \r<br>&gt; normal distribution (for some variable after applying a logit transform).\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; This works well in most cases, but for some data some chains do not manage\r<br>&gt; to estimates the covariance matrix and means of the mvn.\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; At first I thought the problem might be that I have highly correlated data that result \r<br>&gt; in a non-invertible covariance martrix. While I indeed begin to observe \r<br>&gt; problems when the determinant of the cov matrix of the complete data drops from \r<br>&gt; 0.14 to 0.07, the determinant never becomes smaller than 0.006 and some chains always\r<br>&gt; manage to estimate the covariance matrix and means of the mvn. \r<br>&gt; So stan definitively can estimate those parameters, just not consistently \r<br>&gt; with each chain with the model below.\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; It would be nice if anybody could look at the model code below and see if \r<br>&gt; I am doing something wrong.\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; Best - Guido\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; data {\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower = 1&gt; N;      # total N\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower = 1&gt; D;      # number DVs\r<br>&gt;   matrix[N,D] y;         # outcome seperately for DVs\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; logit_transf[D]; # 1 if logit transform is to be applied\r<br>&gt;   int missings[N,D];\r<br>&gt;   int nmiss[D];\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; transformed data{\r<br>&gt;   matrix[N,D] y_normal;\r<br>&gt;   int missing_counter[N,D];\r<br>&gt;   int missingL[N,D];\r<br>&gt;   int imp_idx_start[D];\r<br>&gt;   int imp_idx_end[D];\r<br>&gt;   {\r<br>&gt;     int mc;\r<br>&gt;     mc = 0;\r<br>&gt;     for(v in 1:D) for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;       missingL[n,v] =  missings[n,v] &gt; 0 ? 1 : 0;\r<br>&gt;       mc = missingL[n,v] ? mc+1 : mc;\r<br>&gt;       missing_counter[n,v] =  missingL[n,v] ? mc : 0;\r<br>&gt;       y_normal[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? 0 : (logit_transf[v] ? logit(y[n,v]) : y[n,v]);\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt;   cholesky_factor_corr[D] i_L_Omega; // correlation matrix\r<br>&gt;   vector[D] i_mu;\r<br>&gt;   real imps[sum(nmiss)];\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt;   row_vector[D] y_normal_i[N];\r<br>&gt;   //matrix[N,D] y_i;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   i_mu ~ normal(0,3);\r<br>&gt;   i_L_Omega ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);\r<br>&gt;   imps ~ normal(0,2);\r<br>&gt;   \r<br>&gt;   for (v in 1:D) {\r<br>&gt;     // no missing data\r<br>&gt;     if (nmiss[v] == 0) {\r<br>&gt;       for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;         y_normal_i[n,v] = y_normal[n,v];\r<br>&gt;       } \r<br>&gt;     // missings data\r<br>&gt;     } else {\r<br>&gt;       // imputation on unbounded (normally distributed) scale\r<br>&gt;       for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;         y_normal_i[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? imps[missing_counter[n,v]] : y_normal[n,v];\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt;   // log posterior for imputations\r<br>&gt;   y_normal_i ~ multi_normal_cholesky(i_mu, i_L_Omega);\r<br>&gt; } \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
